From 5a92f5bf74dfed0daea7451e3d4969d479169368 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: y-o-o-z <136268860+y-o-o-z@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 14:44:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] local: history kept across a fe-web password change, wider
 local-erssi check, review fixes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Saving a new fe-web password in the settings derived a new message storage
key but left the open storage on the old one, so the history written so far
was unreadable after the next restart. The stored history is now
re-encrypted with the new key first, in one transaction together with the
messages still waiting in the write queue, and only then is the settings
file written; if re-encrypting fails nothing changes, and if writing the
file fails the history goes back to the old key. Rows the old key cannot
read (left by the earlier key mismatch) are kept as they are instead of
blocking the change. The storage keeps its own copy of the key and zeroes
it on close, independent of the copy the client zeroes on quit. The key
derivation is one function used by login, autoconnect and the settings.

An erssi on this machine was recognised only as localhost, 127.0.0.1 or
::1; other names for it (127.0.1.1 and the rest of 127.0.0.0/8,
::ffff:127.x, the long IPv6 form, "localhost.", the addresses of the
machine's own interfaces) silently lost the certificate pin of
~/.erssi/fe-web-cert.pem. All of them count as local now (host names are
still not resolved). When this machine has an erssi certificate but the
host is not recognised as local, the certificate is still not used, but a
warning says so once, naming caFile and socketPath as the ways to pin it.

caFile and journalDir are read like socketPath: "~/" is the home
directory, a relative path is relative to ~/.erssi (one shared resolver).

A certificate pin that cannot be used (unreadable caFile) is logged at
error level once per distinct message and at debug level while it repeats,
instead of an error line on every reconnect.

The daily session expiry no longer drops the session of a browser that is
attached right now; the timer and quit also record the attached sessions
as used.

Changelog: concurrent requests share one git run, git is killed after 5
seconds, a failure leaves the changelog empty (the help window shows its
"try again" state) and git is asked again after 10 minutes. The patch count
uses the Polish plural forms ("1 łatka", "3 łatki", "27 łatek"; "z 1
łatką", "z 27 łatkami").

Lint: the ESLint errors in the changelog plugin and the Stylelint errors in
the shellter theme are fixed (blank lines only), and one media query in
style.css uses the range notation like the rest of the file.

Tests: the certificate pin and the local-host check, the journal directory
rule, session expiry, re-encryption on a real SQLite database (with a
failing update rolled back) and the changelog plural forms.
